LOGBOOK.md:10:- RUNTIME GATE: Rework 04 keeps complete classification of exact `App/`, `Sources/`, and `relay/` roots and uses language-specific lexical tokens. C-family and Go block comments terminate at the first `*/`; Swift preserves nested comments, escaped identifiers, and raw-string interpolation. C alternative `%:%:` token paste, Swift inferred `.init(contentsOf:)`, and all prior direct/reference/alias/reflection/import forms reject while explicit local `URL(fileURLWithPath:)` reads and documentation-only comments/raw strings remain allowed. Unknown/new kinds, symlinks, and non-regular entries still fail closed.
LOGBOOK.md:11:- CLAIM BOUNDARY: The gate verifies the complete classified repository runtime surface against its enumerated forbidden mechanisms. It is not a general semantic proof for arbitrary future languages; any unclassified file must fail until policy and scanner review.
LOGBOOK.md:12:- VERIFICATION: Twenty-one focused Python tests include compiler/typechecker/preprocessor-backed regressions for all six re-review 04 bypasses plus the prior C macro, escaped-identifier, function-pointer, token-paste, Swift initializer/typealias, Objective-C selector, and Go import cases. The relay-toolchain CI job now checks out full Git history and has a static regression binding that setting to the provenance audit. Supply-chain/asset audits, manifest tests, relay Go tests/vet, unsigned Swift build, formatting/lint, privacy, diff, full-history local-clone audit, and byte-identical double generation are the handoff gates.
LOGBOOK.md:18:- GATE: `make relay-supply-chain-audit` fails on lock/source/recipe/toolchain/license/notice/inventory/provenance/manifest drift, sensitive or workstation data, mutable dependency URLs, and application runtime code-download surfaces. Deterministic generation is byte-idempotent, the focused Python/Swift asset suites pass, and the pinned relay Go tests plus vet remain green.
LOGBOOK.md:29:- ARCHITECTURE: The Release provider is universal `arm64`/`x86_64`, embeds `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter` plus HEV/libssh2 production symbols, contains no `CReluxNativeFixture` or forbidden runtime-loader symbol, links only system frameworks/libraries, and embeds a checksum-valid four-platform relay payload. No signing overlay, embedded private-key payload, signing, installation, launch, VPN preference/tunnel, route, or DNS mutation was used.
LOGBOOK.md:49:- IMPLEMENTATION: The generated workspace now has a minimal macOS host and one embedded packet-tunnel system extension at `Contents/Library/SystemExtensions/works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel.systemextension`. The host and provider use the r12 identifiers, macOS 15 floor, shared build/protocol version, target-owned plists, and separate Development/Developer ID entitlement files. The provider imports NetworkExtension and implements only empty start/stop plus version-message handling.
LOGBOOK.md:53:- BUILD GRAPH: The minimal provider intentionally depends on `ReluxTunnelCore`, not `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter`. Adding the adapter exposed an existing SwiftPM/Xcode transitive binary-output collision: both `ReluxLibSSH2` and `CReluxNativeFixture` produce `include/module.modulemap`. Packet and transport implementation is out of this task's scope; the later runtime-integration task owns resolving that collision before adding the adapter dependency.
LOGBOOK.md:85:- COVERAGE: Host-before-auth rejection variants, approved algorithms and opaque signing, direct/exec/upload isolation, bounded EAGAIN/backpressure, byte/time/manual/server rekey traffic, keepalive scheduling/failure, 15 cleanup scopes, complete available metrics, privacy-safe errors, resource baselines, and every deferred runtime surface are asserted. Exact M3 ownership remains `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
LOGBOOK.md:153:- DEFERRED GAPS: Receive-window consumer credit/cap, RFC channel-open reasons, exact exec-exit/core-dump metadata, and deep server-rekey/keepalive observability remain explicit `unsupported` or runtime `notReported`, owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`. Physical scale, memory, and soak remain separate M3 rows; no exact value was inferred from the C fixture's 16 observed server-KEX transitions.
LOGBOOK.md:159:- IMPLEMENTATION: The macOS profile-driven SSH boundary now projects typed profile, path, connect, negotiation, host-policy, Keychain, public-key authentication, cancellation/user-stop, and session-close causes into the fixed `sshProfileBootstrap` provider domain. Public errors and runtime snapshots carry only stable code/stage, configuration generation, user-action category, retry disposition, and reviewed algorithm/endpoint-family context; arbitrary underlying prose and identifying values are structurally unrepresentable.
LOGBOOK.md:162:- VERIFICATION: Golden taxonomy, hostile-text/prohibited-data, cancellation, NSError redaction, socket/URL, NWError, OSStatus, runtime-snapshot, selected libssh2 timeout, and live authentication-timeout tests pass. Recursive Swift format lint, boundary/native/libssh2 verification, all 425 Swift tests in 35 suites, `swift build`, and `git diff --check` exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:172:### 1838 — macOS provider resolves exact system-domain Keychain credentials (TASK-260715-1o9wjz)
LOGBOOK.md:173:- IMPLEMENTATION: The macOS provider now resolves the file-based system-domain Keychain with `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(.system, ...)` and performs one read-only generic-password lookup using an explicit one-Keychain `kSecMatchSearchList`, fixed non-identifying service, exact canonical credential reference, `kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain=false`, and match limit one. The query surface has no hard-coded Keychain path, ambient search, enumeration, fallback, add/update/delete operation, access group, or accessibility attribute.
LOGBOOK.md:176:- VERIFICATION: Eleven focused resolver tests include a real two-throwaway-Keychain positive/negative search-list boundary with placeholder-only bytes; 56 combined resolver/SSH/provider tests and the complete 403-test/33-suite run pass. `make validate-core`, recursive Swift format lint, core boundaries, static forbidden-surface scan, build, and diff checks all exit 0. The required deprecated file-based `SecKeychain` API emits SDK deprecation warnings but is the accepted daemon/system-domain mechanism.
LOGBOOK.md:182:- GENERATION SAFETY: A fresh `SSHProfileRuntimeSnapshotLoader` owns one runtime start. A present exact five-field v1 start request binds `configurationGeneration` to the SHA-256 digest of the exact stored canonical snapshot bytes before first capture; nil options may capture the validated stored value. Byte-identical rereads return the original capture, while stale requests, same-generation field replacement, newer-generation replacement, and concurrent competing captures fail with the fixed privacy-safe `profileGenerationMismatch` error.
LOGBOOK.md:184:- VERIFICATION: Thirteen focused loader tests, 52 combined loader/runtime-start/session tests, and the complete 392-test/32-suite run pass, including HEV. `swift build`, recursive format lint, core-boundary validation, and `git diff --check` all exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:188:- GATE DISPOSITION (supersedes the 2026-08-11 blocked reading): Overall macOS-only Gate P0 is PASS. Accepted C1 authority for the Relux Works r12 matrix, exact server-issued device-bound profiles, signed archive inspection, system approval, and physical provider lifecycle directly close every in-scope technical provisioning row. The `TASK-260715-apc34w` membership/agreement/Admin-field gaps remain release/account-assurance obligations and are not inferred closed, but the owner-approved active prototype scope does not treat them as unresolved P0 portal approval.
LOGBOOK.md:190:- REVALIDATION: The four Mac Development profiles expire on 2027-08-10. Any profile or agreement/membership change affecting portal use, device or OS change, signing/capability/entitlement edit, system-approval reset, or identifier/archive drift requires the affected readiness, provisioning, archive, and physical lifecycle gates to be rerun. Agreement effective/expiry dates remain uncaptured and must be recorded before any release gate that depends on them.
LOGBOOK.md:212:- PORTAL RESULT: Xcode automatic provisioning created or updated exactly the four r12 macOS identifiers and downloaded one device-bound Mac Team Provisioning Profile for each: `works.relux.tunnel.mac`, `.mac.tunnel`, `.probe.mac`, and `.probe.mac.tunnel`. Network Extensions is profile-authorized on hosts and providers alike; no portal reauthentication or two-factor challenge occurred.
LOGBOOK.md:215:- ANOMALY: The portal/profile phase succeeded, but final local codesigning returned `errSecInternalComponent` for each harness target because unattended signing-key access was unavailable. A separate all-target no-sign compile succeeded. Read-only Safari automation also remained unavailable (timeout exit 142), consistent with APC34W-B5; neither issue caused a portal reauthentication challenge or invalidated the downloaded profiles.
LOGBOOK.md:353:- FINDING: what makes a pre-authorization mean anything is that one **exact** exception was reviewed. A field that can move without failing a gate is not the field that was reviewed, so the pre-authorization launders whatever the field becomes: a broadened path grants unreviewed filesystem access, a missing channel breaks the shipping provider on the same OS policy, a different arming owner severs the evidence chain.
LOGBOOK.md:366:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:373:- DECISION: owner is **`TASK-260715-1uxx3i`** — an **existing** task. Its `macos-15` minimum lane already exists in the deployment-target policy and reading the runner's own `application.sb` is a credential-free static read inside its AC2; it already reaches this contract transitively via `TASK-260715-uyju7n`, so **no board element was created** and no edge added. `TASK-260715-1o9wjz` consumes the same answer at runtime. Tracked as **OC-5**.
LOGBOOK.md:377:- FINDING: `add_resource` validates against the **filesystem** and `update_resource` against the **registry**, so a file written directly into `.task-board/.resources/<TASK>/` is "already exists" to one and "not found" to the other. Stage new resources **outside** the resource dir and add by `path`.
LOGBOOK.md:439:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 withheld `keychain-access-groups` from `macos.provider` on rule **K1** (the root/system-domain keychain has no access groups) and kept it `required` on `macos.host` for "the host's own vault plus a pre-seed staging item". Reviewer verdict 04 F1: neither half needs a *shared* group. The real defect is narrower and more generalisable than "an unused entitlement" — **K1 is written about the provider and is silent on the host**, so the host row was never tested at all. A rule scoped to one row reads like coverage of the class it names. New rule **K2** covers the host case, and K1 now carries a `hostScope` clause admitting what it does not govern; R31 fails if that clause is removed.
LOGBOOK.md:441:- FINDING: Withholding costs the host nothing, and Apple documents it rather than it being inferred. *Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps*: the `com.apple.application-identifier` entitlement is added to **every** app at signing; "the system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's **default** keychain access group"; items in it are "**private to** App One"; "if you don't specify any keychain access groups, then **the app ID is the default**." TN3137 adds the macOS half: the access-group list is built from code-signing entitlements "**authorized by a provisioning profile**" — and `macos.host` is provisioned on both channels, so the injected identifier qualifies.
LOGBOOK.md:454:- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
LOGBOOK.md:467:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
LOGBOOK.md:472:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:480:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
LOGBOOK.md:491:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
LOGBOOK.md:496:- FINDING: Read `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` off three shipping Developer-ID host/provider pairs on this Mac: hosts `false` in all three (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), providers `true` in all three. Apple requires the **provider system extension** to be sandboxed; the containing app is not covered by that rule for direct distribution. r1 attributed both to a single Apple requirement.
LOGBOOK.md:497:- DECISION: `ReluxProxyMac`'s sandbox is recorded as a **project** decision — `.spec/platform-distribution.md` embeds Sparkle only in the sandboxed hardened-runtime host with `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach exceptions, per ADR-018 — not an Apple rule. The contract now carries `requirementSource` per row (`apple-requirement` / `project-architecture` / `sandbox-consequence` / `platform-inherent`) and validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple requirement.
LOGBOOK.md:498:- NOTE: This sharpens OC-2. None of the three shipping products is a sandboxed host, so the sandboxed-host + `system-extension.install` combination has **no local precedent** to lean on; `9yp8to` is genuinely first through that door.
LOGBOOK.md:513:- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
LOGBOOK.md:516:- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
LOGBOOK.md:521:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
LOGBOOK.md:531:- ROOT CAUSE: `TunnelProviderAdapter.providerDidFail` was `nonisolated` and deferred *all* admission into a freshly spawned unstructured `Task`. Swift orders nothing between unstructured tasks contending for the same actor, so two sequential provider-failure callbacks could enter `handleProviderFailure` in reverse order. The `cancelTunnelIssuedGeneration` ledger enforced exactly-once but had no notion of *which* call arrived first, so the second error (`internalInvariant` 1009) beat the first (`runtimeStartupFailed` 1007) in **10 of 40** focused runs (~25%).
LOGBOOK.md:552:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:565:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 deliberately includes the Gate P0 system-VPN approval click. The disposable probe builds in minutes from profiles created minutes earlier, so it stays one sitting. Hold H2 (real app, different bundle identifier) cannot be folded in and remains the next human boundary after 202 autonomous tasks.
LOGBOOK.md:601:- EVIDENCE: Positive schema fixtures cover three paired repetitions, m=3 as exact `59/60` with ranks `83/9916`, and a production-authorized reviewed row. Hostile fixtures reject missing review/statistics/lineage plus unavailable or invalid-environment rows claiming `measured-pass`. Cross-row hash/sort/pair/effect/filesystem/time semantics are bound to `TASK-260721-2ohf99` by a new task-scoped precondition.
LOGBOOK.md:613:- EVIDENCE: A valid row and equality-boundary fixture pass schema v1; aggregate DNS one-over and reviewer-hostile overlays fail. Non-expressible hash/ID, clock ordering, artifact/filesystem, gate/counter, replay/statistics, and immutable-update rules are explicitly assigned to TASK-260721-2ohf99 with the fixtures attached as preconditions.
LOGBOOK.md:624:### 2305 — macOS self-update is exact, signed-feed, forward-only, and system-extension gated (TASK-260717-2uyfn5)
LOGBOOK.md:625:- DECISION: Pin official Sparkle 2.9.4 exactly in the macOS host; require pre-extraction EdDSA verification plus a signed feed with failure expiration disabled; publish only final Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime, notarized/stapled DMGs from a public read-only HTTPS origin after asset-first verification. Stable is Sparkle's default channel and opt-in prerelease uses `prerelease`; private GitHub remains manual distribution.
LOGBOOK.md:627:- PLATFORM: Apple TN3134 requires the directly distributed macOS packet tunnel to be a system extension. Sparkle host replacement/relaunch does not activate the new provider; the host must stop the tunnel, relaunch, submit a separate replacement request, surface approval/restart state, and avoid automatic reconnect. Exact OS behavior remains a physical gate under `TASK-260715-1r48pc` and `TASK-260715-2aessv`.
LOGBOOK.md:632:- DECISION: Security copy now carries stable claim IDs and one of four evidence boundaries: implemented component, contract implemented/integration gated, accepted design/release gated, or planned/unverified. No positive system-VPN claim is authorized as a verified release claim until candidate-specific composition and physical evidence is accepted.
LOGBOOK.md:634:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
LOGBOOK.md:643:- SECURITY: Identity is one canonical JSON line capped at 512 bytes. It validates the injected semantic version and lowercase 40-hex commit, hashes the running executable through `/proc/self/exe` on Linux or the cgo-free standard executable path on Darwin, and keeps all diagnostics on stderr. Protocol stdout has one writer and contains only hello/framed bytes; the production dependency graph includes neither `net` nor `os/exec` and creates no runtime files.
LOGBOOK.md:648:- DECISION: `relay/toolchain-manifest-v1.json` is the checked-in authority for Go 1.26.5 `gc`/internal-linker identity, official Go and Syft host archive hashes, the standard-library-only module lock, license hashes/identifiers, four target/CPU baselines, the absence of build SDKs/sysroots, macOS 12.0, deterministic flags, and the exact actions/checkout v7.0.1 revision. Linux static linkage does not justify a kernel-version floor; the declared runtime baseline is native unprivileged Ubuntu 24.04 on both x86_64 and arm64 CI fixtures.
LOGBOOK.md:649:- SECURITY: Target builds construct an environment allowlist and isolate `HOME`, `TMPDIR`, `GOCACHE`, `GOMODCACHE`, and `GOPATH` per target below `.build/relay/work`; `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `GOPROXY=off`, `GOSUMDB=off`, `GOVCS=off`, `GOENV=off`, and `GOWORK=off` prevent undeclared downloads or workstation credential/config reuse.
LOGBOOK.md:651:- REWORK: Provenance now compares every installed Go directory/file, mode, and content digest to the checksum-pinned archive and rejects missing, added, changed, duplicate, traversing, symlink, hardlink, device, or other unsafe entries. Exact negative fixtures cover runtime and standard-library tampering, missing Go, missing `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, checkout pin drift, and `GOAMD64=v1`/`GOARM64=v8.0` metadata drift.
LOGBOOK.md:677:- ADDRESS: Resolver results with a non-empty IPv6 zone are discarded before unmapping, accepted-result byte credit, family/socket admission, or send. An all-scoped result set closes its original bounded reservation with zero descriptors/sends and zero retained scheduler/registry resources; a later in-cap unzoned IPv6 result is selected byte-exactly without zone rewriting. The system socket seam also rejects zoned IPv6 defensively.
LOGBOOK.md:733:- RETRY/REASON: T15 is the sole same-runtime re-entry after exhaustion or a local-change-required relay result. It accepts only a higher locally authorized relay-only policy generation on the current healthy base, allocates a fresh bounded recovery cycle, and cannot accept remote/UI input or overlap M3 path/host/route/lane/sleep/NAT64/captive ownership. Successful startup activation publishes local finite `activation_ready` with all capabilities false until atomic full publication changes the reason to `none`.
LOGBOOK.md:745:- GENERATION/PRIVACY: Runtime generation remains M1-owned, transport generation is M3-owned, and relay attempt/active generation is M2-owned. Association identity cannot cross that tuple. Schema-2 runtime/capability snapshots add finite reasons, retry owner, generations, and locally validated build/limit evidence while preserving explicit schema-1 responses; remote text and sensitive traffic identifiers remain structurally excluded.
LOGBOOK.md:746:- STOP-LINE GATES: The contract is implementable/testable against injected fakes, but production full/degraded readiness remains false until the accepted M0 composition manifest and a production-authorized DNS runtime policy exist. Current DNS numeric candidates are evidence only. No engine, MTU, lane/window, timing, or overlap value was selected here.
LOGBOOK.md:760:- AUTHORITY: The canonical artifact class is `non-authoritative-candidate`; `productionAuthorization.permitted` and `adr022MayAdvanceToAccepted` remain false. Exact blockers are `TASK-260715-1gjxer` and `TASK-260715-1pn983`, and selected-engine/physical provider rows remain a declared later external gate. No production DNS runtime or candidate numeric value changed.
LOGBOOK.md:773:- REVIEW/REVALIDATION: ADR-022 remains Proposed and every downstream consumer remains blocked pending independent architecture acceptance. Selected-SSH, physical-iPhone, HEV/OS/toolchain, relay, diagnostics, and policy drift are explicit revalidation triggers. Evidence: `.research/260721_task-260721-3miqh4-dns-runtime-policy-v1.md`, machine vectors and raw summaries under `.research/`, harness `scripts/dns-policy-evidence.py`.
LOGBOOK.md:778:- FAILURE OWNERSHIP: One runtime generation owns one active endpoint and one reusable TCP connection epoch. EOF, timeout, framing/correlation, partial-write ambiguity, channel, or SSH failure atomically retires the epoch, classifies all in-flight queries, and permits one coordinator to replay only eligible QUERY operations as one ordered batch at the next endpoint. Cancelled/expired/terminal work does not retry; late old-epoch callbacks cannot answer; promotion clears cache/transport generation. A valid response including any RCODE never shops resolvers.
LOGBOOK.md:809:- INTEGRATION: A freshly loaded exact-owned but disabled manager correctly failed inside `OwnedVPNManagerRepository`, but the controller initially leaked the repository error type. The start preflight now normalizes that one repository result to `VPNSessionControllerError.configurationDisabled` without invoking the system start API or duplicating ownership logic.
LOGBOOK.md:810:- COVERAGE: An injectable boundary matrix exercises the three public start errors plus an unknown exact domain/code independently through both Apple adapter modules. The real repository-to-controller disabled path asserts zero system start calls and zero manager mutations.
LOGBOOK.md:818:### 0511 — Host VPN control keeps system and provider authorities independent (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
LOGBOOK.md:820:- ORDERING: Start and stop share one locked once-only stop claim, so cancellation, timeout, and a concurrent explicit stop cannot duplicate `stopTunnel`. Controller retirement cancels host waits and observers without claiming that stop; a cancelled stop waiter does not cancel the joined system-stop observation for other callers.
LOGBOOK.md:821:- PROJECTION: Every non-connected system status clears capability immediately. A connected projection accepts only correlated, negotiated lifecycle/capability responses at one matching position strictly newer than the last accepted generation/sequence; relaunch starts without historical app state and asks the provider again. Non-running provider lifecycle states fail safe to zero capabilities.
LOGBOOK.md:822:- ERROR CONTRACT: All 19 public `NEVPNConnectionError` codes, provider codes 1001–1009, unknown domains/codes, provider unknown codes, nil errors, and timeout/cancellation unavailability map to stable finite reasons while system status remains authoritative.
LOGBOOK.md:851:- CLEANUP: Every generation receives a fixed 64-handle cleanup registry. Stop immediately rejects work and fans out cancellation, then joins one reverse runtime stop behind an injected ten-second budget. Expiry force-closes registered controllable handles and records only the raw Apple reason, one fixed counter/gauge, and the finite `cleanup_deadline_exceeded` token. Provider fatal failure emits one stable 1001–1009 NSError and the later system stop joins the same cleanup.
LOGBOOK.md:852:- ORDERING: Stop during factory/runtime startup wins, retires late-created handles, and completes the pending Apple start gate before each stop gate. The 60-second startup race and cleanup race use injected clocks and continuations; no correctness sleep or unbounded task-group join is used.
LOGBOOK.md:856:### 0245 — VPN lifecycle separates ownership, enablement, system state, and runtime capability (TASK-260715-1q4qhw)
LOGBOOK.md:858:- PLATFORM FINDING: Apple limits `loadAllFromPreferences` to configurations created by the calling app, but enabling an enterprise VPN can cause the OS to disable another app's enterprise VPN. Therefore ordinary ensure preserves/creates a disabled configuration and explicit foreground user intent owns enable/save/reload. Relux never mutates the unrelated manager object and reports the system-level enable effect truthfully.
LOGBOOK.md:859:- AUTHORITY: `NETunnelProviderSession.status` is the sole system-session authority; a fresh generation/sequence provider snapshot is the sole runtime capability authority. Host suspension/termination removes observers only. Provider start/stop once-gates and exact deadlines remain extension-owned and join the accepted coordinator cleanup.
LOGBOOK.md:896:- CONCURRENCY: Each recorder is stamped with its runtime generation. Advancing the store resets all aggregates and snapshot sequence, and late callbacks through an older recorder become no-ops. Component updates use the bounded non-blocking ingestion lane recorded at 0053; immutable snapshots are encoded only after leaving that lane.
LOGBOOK.md:906:- VERIFICATION: Focused runtime codec tests and `make validate-core` pass; the full gate reports 183 tests in 21 suites plus boundary/native-package checks and a post-test build. Repository-wide Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and `task-board validate` are clean.
LOGBOOK.md:910:- SECURITY: The prior `TunnelConfiguration.parameters: [String:String]` escape hatch made private-key/passphrase exclusion structurally impossible. It was removed; provider/start configuration now contains only a schema-v1 UUID-backed profile reference, while configuration snapshots use distinct UUID-backed profile revision, credential, and trust references. Harness experiment parameters remain in harness-only recorded output and no longer cross into the runtime configuration.
LOGBOOK.md:914:### 2317 — M1 runtime contract separates structural work from production M0 binding (TASK-260715-30zng6)
LOGBOOK.md:915:- DECISION: The packet-tunnel extension owns one actor-serialized runtime generation, with immutable app-message snapshots and no live forwarding dependency on the containing app. Startup is configuration -> verified/authenticated SSH -> TCP/DNS readiness -> pure packet preflight -> settings apply -> existing PacketBridge.start/read activation -> TCP + safe-DNS publication; every failure rolls back through one idempotent, non-cancellable stopping path.
LOGBOOK.md:966:- MILESTONE: One authority now generates both bindings: `Protocol/Relay/relay-v1.schema.json` → `scripts/relay-protocol-tool.py` → `Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/RelayProtocol/Generated/RelayProtocolV1+Generated.swift` + `relay/internal/protocol/generated_v1.go` (checked in, runtime-parse-free). `make relay-protocol-generate` / `make relay-protocol-check` per the 111tde binding; all values verbatim from 111tde/18owh7. Ready for review.
LOGBOOK.md:993:- DECISION: Canonical JSON at `Protocol/Relay/relay-v1.schema.json` generates checked-in Swift/Go values only; handwritten Swift in `ReluxTunnelCore/RelayProtocol` and Go in `relay/internal/protocol` own behavior. `make relay-protocol-generate` is the sole regeneration command and `make relay-protocol-check` owns deterministic double-generation, drift/manual-edit failure, compilation, and schema-negative gates. No Swift/Go FFI or runtime schema parser is permitted.
LOGBOOK.md:1055:- ROOT CAUSE: `HEVDescriptorBorrowHandle.waitForReturn()` joins HEV and only then calls `runtime.statistics()` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter/HEVIntegration.swift:409-423`). Pinned unmodified HEV calls `hev_socks5_tunnel_fini()` before `hev_socks5_tunnel_main_from_str()` returns, and that fini resets all four `stat_tx_*`/`stat_rx_*` globals to zero (`src/hev-socks5-tunnel.c:657-671`). The public stats call after join therefore cannot observe completed-run traffic.
LOGBOOK.md:1057:- OPTIONS: Preserve unmodified HEV by capturing statistics while main is still active (immediately before quit, plus an explicit strategy for spontaneous return), add a wrapper-owned live snapshot to the native runtime seam, or explicitly remove completed-run HEV counters from the acceptance contract. Recommendation: retain the counters and revise the runtime seam to snapshot before HEV fini; do not weaken the integration assertions or patch the pinned HEV binary.
LOGBOOK.md:1127:### 2220 — Four relay assets built; native runtime and budget gates remain external (TASK-260715-24icoz)
LOGBOOK.md:1128:- IMPLEMENTATION: Added `relay-portable-assets` / `inspect-assets`, which requires a caller-owned total bundle budget and emits a path-free four-asset report only after exact layout/name, regular executable mode, raw format/machine, Go CPU metadata, linkage, minimum runtime, dynamic-library, debug-section, size, SHA-256, and privacy checks. An over-budget report is retained before the command fails; no default budget silently creates product policy.
LOGBOOK.md:1155:- MILESTONE: Audit accepted → `done`. Every load-bearing claim reproduced from upstream: tag→commit pins for both candidates plus all six transitive deps (ls-remote), all three archive SHA-256 and both license hashes (fresh downloads), 16 MiB window `SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift:211`, internal `_rekey()` `NIOSSHHandler.swift:511`, libssh2 window APIs and direct-tcpip hardcode `src/channel.c:384,457`, unexported reexchange `src/packet.c:1369`, version-macro anomaly, six CVE fix commits confirmed ancestors of `a343024`, GHSA advisories via API, exactly 679 libssh2 commits/12mo.
